[SPEAKER_01]: So, for as long as the mind doesn't, again, start delighting in essential thoughts,

346
00:37:52,936 --> 00:37:59,339
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, so then, well, then you're overlooking some other background attitude.

347
00:37:59,359 --> 00:37:59,880
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, exactly.

348
00:37:59,900 --> 00:38:02,101
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're not seeing now the attitude of expectation.

349
00:38:02,121 --> 00:38:05,483
[SPEAKER_01]: You're just running towards this experience you think you should have.

350
00:38:05,663 --> 00:38:05,883
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

351
00:38:06,143 --> 00:38:07,664
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's actually the suffering.

352
00:38:07,844 --> 00:38:08,084
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

353
00:38:08,924 --> 00:38:13,847
[SPEAKER_01]: Whether the experience comes later on or whatever, you still didn't understand.

354
00:38:15,453 --> 00:38:17,434
[SPEAKER_01]: Initially, you were suffering to begin with.

355
00:38:18,115 --> 00:38:22,177
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not really solving the liabilities of suffering.

356
00:38:23,058 --> 00:38:24,459
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's it.

357
00:38:24,499 --> 00:38:27,921
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you notice the sign of the mind.

358
00:38:28,121 --> 00:38:29,742
[SPEAKER_01]: You notice the inclination of the mind.

359
00:38:30,142 --> 00:38:33,944
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why it then said, the suttas would then say, your job is then not to get rid of it.

360
00:38:34,485 --> 00:38:37,987
[SPEAKER_01]: As the satipaṭṭhāna sutta itself says, actually, he knows

361
00:38:38,803 --> 00:38:40,344
[SPEAKER_01]: Lustful mind is lustful mind.

362
00:38:40,384 --> 00:38:42,185
[SPEAKER_01]: He knows averse mind is averse mind.

363
00:38:42,205 --> 00:38:44,427
[SPEAKER_01]: He knows distracted mind is distracted mind.

364
00:38:45,107 --> 00:38:45,447
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

365
00:38:46,648 --> 00:38:48,729
[SPEAKER_01]: And... Yeah, that's defined as purity, actually.

366
00:38:48,769 --> 00:38:49,470
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah.

367
00:38:49,790 --> 00:39:03,659
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you... you basically... for as long as you don't lose sight of that, and you will lose sight of that when you either fall again back into the sense objects, or

368
00:39:04,558 --> 00:39:13,345
[SPEAKER_01]: You fall too much into the side of denying them and trying to replace them with other thoughts that you think are better.

369
00:39:14,185 --> 00:39:20,830
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to stay in the middle, which is, you're not delighting in the thoughts, but you're also not trying to replace them with others.

370
00:39:21,251 --> 00:39:23,773
[SPEAKER_01]: And, to that extent, you're seeing the side of the mind.

371
00:39:23,833 --> 00:39:27,335
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why it's inextricably tied the middle way.

372
00:39:28,737 --> 00:39:30,378
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not getting rid of the mind,

373
00:39:31,645 --> 00:39:37,828
[SPEAKER_01]: It's knowing it for what it is, and then not adding fuel to the unwholesome states that are there.

374
00:39:38,348 --> 00:39:39,249
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

375
00:39:39,329 --> 00:39:44,091
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, when the mind is in the... it obviously works the opposite way as well.

376
00:39:44,111 --> 00:39:51,555
[SPEAKER_01]: When the mind is in a wholesome state, let's say, when the five energies have been abandoned, you also know that for yourself, and you know exactly how it came about.

377
00:39:51,675 --> 00:39:52,856
[SPEAKER_01]: It didn't happen by accident.

378
00:39:53,236 --> 00:39:58,559
[SPEAKER_01]: It's because the whole time you were aware of the king's preferences, and you were doing the right thing.

379
00:40:00,520 --> 00:40:00,920
[SPEAKER_01]: And now,

380
00:40:03,831 --> 00:40:13,795
[SPEAKER_01]: The king is pleased, and you know exactly how to maintain that, and there is nobody in the world that can suddenly make you lose that which you worked for.

381
00:40:14,116 --> 00:40:19,938
[SPEAKER_01]: So, like, when people say, oh, I lost my samādhi or something, that's because they didn't have it to begin with.

382
00:40:20,238 --> 00:40:21,559
[SPEAKER_01]: If you see the signs in the mind,

383
00:40:22,143 --> 00:40:25,765
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no way except your own intention.

384
00:40:26,646 --> 00:40:36,832
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, if it is right samādhi to begin with, which happens on the basis of seeing the side of the mind, there is nothing except your own intention which can, you know, make it decrease.

385
00:40:39,234 --> 00:40:50,821
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the... you could basically say, as a kind of illustration, slightly

386
00:40:56,045 --> 00:41:05,593
[SPEAKER_01]: Wherever you're looking at, even mentally, whenever you're directing your beam of attention, if you want to put it that way, the side of the mind is what's around that.

387
00:41:05,913 --> 00:41:06,934
[SPEAKER_01]: Around that.

388
00:41:06,954 --> 00:41:17,403
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the same way I'm looking here now, and the back of my head, like, I know that there's stuff back there, but I can't directly look at it.

389
00:41:18,023 --> 00:41:23,448
[SPEAKER_01]: And, while I'm looking at outside, I know that there's stuff back there.

390
00:41:24,591 --> 00:41:30,072
[SPEAKER_01]: I might know, let's say, that, you know, there's something behind my head, and if I lean too far back, I'm going to hit my head.

391
00:41:30,132 --> 00:41:31,512
[SPEAKER_01]: But I'm not directly looking at it.

392
00:41:31,652 --> 00:41:31,852
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

393
00:41:32,272 --> 00:41:41,294
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's... that could be one practical way of... of... because if you try to turn towards it, well, now, see, the back of my head remains the back of my head.

394
00:41:41,354 --> 00:41:42,134
[SPEAKER_01]: It will never change.

395
00:41:42,334 --> 00:41:45,074
[SPEAKER_01]: So, is this the recollection and awareness aspect?

396
00:41:45,435 --> 00:41:45,635
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

397
00:41:45,675 --> 00:41:47,795
[SPEAKER_01]: So, actually, that's another thing that says it.

398
00:41:47,855 --> 00:41:53,636
[SPEAKER_01]: Without seeing the center of the mind, you don't have recollection and awareness, even though you might be...

399
00:42:04,430 --> 00:42:07,452
[SPEAKER_01]: you don't see the side of the mind, you don't have recollection and awareness.

400
00:42:07,532 --> 00:42:07,732
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

401
00:42:07,812 --> 00:42:09,873
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's not awareness of... like a...

402
00:42:10,053 --> 00:42:18,778
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, it's even a... like a... like a... it doesn't even make sense to call observation of one object awareness.

403
00:42:18,958 --> 00:42:19,158
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

404
00:42:19,398 --> 00:42:21,539
[SPEAKER_01]: Because when the suttas say, what is awareness?

405
00:42:21,600 --> 00:42:30,905
[SPEAKER_01]: It says, a monk is aware of his thoughts as they arise, as they endure, as they cease, and his feelings as they arise, as they endure, as they cease.

406
00:42:31,265 --> 00:42:33,266
[SPEAKER_01]: So, can you actually see those things as objects?

407
00:42:35,362 --> 00:42:38,805
[SPEAKER_01]: The whole point is, no, you can't, because those things are...

408
00:42:42,628 --> 00:42:52,635
[SPEAKER_01]: In order to... you can see the thoughts themselves are objects, of course, but your knowledge of those things is not on the level of objects.

409
00:42:52,695 --> 00:42:53,196
[SPEAKER_01]: It cannot be.

410
00:42:53,336 --> 00:42:58,740
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's a general principle of knowledge, in general.

411
00:42:58,760 --> 00:43:02,283
[SPEAKER_01]: How does a car mechanic

412
00:43:03,144 --> 00:43:05,306
[SPEAKER_01]: I think I might have given that example before.

413
00:43:05,366 --> 00:43:12,292
[SPEAKER_01]: He knows all the inner workings of the car and so on.

414
00:43:12,533 --> 00:43:14,454
[SPEAKER_01]: That knowledge is not a sense object.

415
00:43:14,995 --> 00:43:21,000
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if it were, you just show that to somebody else and he would have exactly the same knowledge the car mechanic does.

416
00:43:21,080 --> 00:43:22,081
[SPEAKER_01]: But it doesn't work like that.

417
00:43:23,202 --> 00:43:25,364
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's

418
00:43:37,093 --> 00:43:41,074
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yeah, that's one practical way of thinking about it.

419
00:43:41,134 --> 00:43:43,716
[SPEAKER_01]: It's always behind your attention.

420
00:43:43,776 --> 00:43:45,036
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the principle of the mind.

421
00:43:45,636 --> 00:43:46,777
[SPEAKER_01]: But it is a phenomenon.

422
00:43:46,937 --> 00:43:57,582
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's important not to mystify it and think that it's not a phenomenon or it's hidden in my experience or something.

423
00:43:57,662 --> 00:43:58,162
[SPEAKER_01]: No, it's there.

424
00:44:01,762 --> 00:44:04,584
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not like the back of my head doesn't exist, or like what's behind me doesn't exist.

425
00:44:05,124 --> 00:44:05,644
[SPEAKER_01]: It is there.

426
00:44:06,044 --> 00:44:09,846
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just that if I focus too much on what's in front of me, I forget.

427
00:44:10,227 --> 00:44:12,668
[SPEAKER_00]: You cannot sustain that knowledge of the backgrounds.

428
00:44:13,268 --> 00:44:18,451
[SPEAKER_00]: So, to the extent that you lose yourself in all this in front, you will be compromising that background knowledge, basically.

429
00:44:18,471 --> 00:44:18,691
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

430
00:44:18,851 --> 00:44:27,356
[SPEAKER_01]: And obviously, in front can be on the most basic level sights, sounds, smells, tastes, touches, but you can even

431
00:44:28,072 --> 00:44:38,457
[SPEAKER_01]: In front would even count as, you know, your ideas about the dhamma, and your notions, and your effort towards it, and your striving.

432
00:44:38,557 --> 00:44:39,558
[SPEAKER_01]: That becomes in front.

433
00:44:40,138 --> 00:44:52,664
[SPEAKER_01]: And then behind that, there's a whole range of attitudes, and cravings, and expectations, that if you're not actively making an effort to recognize, they will be driving that whole endeavor, and means

434
00:44:55,429 --> 00:44:57,090
[SPEAKER_01]: It's content might be present there.

435
00:44:57,170 --> 00:44:58,111
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not actually done.

436
00:44:58,371 --> 00:45:03,055
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, because there's a relationship between mind, citta, and cetanā, which means intention.

437
00:45:03,095 --> 00:45:03,615
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, exactly.

438
00:45:03,635 --> 00:45:05,937
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yeah, they're actually derived from the same root.

439
00:45:05,997 --> 00:45:22,409
[SPEAKER_01]: So, mind really is... it's... that's why, again, it's also not just something you catch as some sort of independent object, like you would catch a vision of something.

440
00:45:29,543 --> 00:45:44,388
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not the best simile, because it's kind of instantaneous, whereas I've been saying the side of the mind is something that's developed gradually.

441
00:45:44,429 --> 00:45:50,391
[SPEAKER_01]: You will never have a sudden insight where your recognition goes from 10% to 90% in one night.

442
00:45:50,431 --> 00:45:51,071
[SPEAKER_01]: It's impossible.

443
00:45:51,791 --> 00:45:57,133
[SPEAKER_01]: But it serves to illustrate with, like, you know, you see your nails,

444
00:46:02,897 --> 00:46:15,329
[SPEAKER_01]: In your fingers and stuff, and, you know, they're kind of very... you see that even though you're not cutting your nails, for some reason, they're always very short.

445
00:46:17,050 --> 00:46:21,915
[SPEAKER_01]: And, at some point, you figure out that you catch yourself biting your nails.

446
00:46:22,656 --> 00:46:26,159
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why, actually, your nails were in that condition.

447
00:46:26,599 --> 00:46:27,220
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's the same.

448
00:46:30,498 --> 00:46:33,039
[SPEAKER_01]: You're self-experiencing the suffering as a symptom.

449
00:46:33,980 --> 00:46:35,160
[SPEAKER_01]: And what's causing that?

450
00:46:35,941 --> 00:46:37,781
[SPEAKER_01]: It's these intentions that you don't see.

451
00:46:39,302 --> 00:46:42,044
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's where the sign of the mind is.

452
00:46:43,184 --> 00:46:57,291
[SPEAKER_01]: And it would be... slowly, you get to see, through the evolution of the gradual training... See, that sign of the mind is... has to be present throughout, even from the very start with the virtue.

453
00:46:57,993 --> 00:47:00,535
[SPEAKER_01]: But the extent of it is getting refined.

454
00:47:00,775 --> 00:47:12,181
[SPEAKER_01]: So, even just to keep the five precepts, like, to keep them in their spirits, you can keep the rules, you know, the letter of the rules, mechanically.

455
00:47:12,402 --> 00:47:23,448
[SPEAKER_01]: But to be able to keep the rules in their spirit, you would already have to have a basic recognition of the side of the mind, because, you know, let's say, lying.

456
00:47:24,088 --> 00:47:26,810
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, if you don't discern your intention,

457
00:47:29,462 --> 00:47:42,406
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when you're saying what you're saying to another person, there might be cases where you're not necessarily telling an outright lie, but you're intentionally misrepresenting the truth, because it's just too unpleasant to say.

458
00:47:43,146 --> 00:47:52,249
[SPEAKER_01]: So, or, you know, you might be keeping even, let's say, the eight precepts, and the precept on entertainment, let's say.

459
00:47:52,769 --> 00:47:58,111
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, you might, in various occasions, come across

460
00:48:00,737 --> 00:48:28,244
[SPEAKER_01]: News, whatever, videos, newspapers and stuff, or you might be using the internet for some practical purpose, but unless you're able to see that intention behind, so why is it that I'm actually choosing to do this right now, you would be unable to really know whether you're actually refraining from entertainment or not, because anything can become entertainment, just like anything can be

461
00:48:30,688 --> 00:48:31,889
[SPEAKER_01]: A way of distracting yourself.

462
00:48:32,189 --> 00:48:34,371
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then, sense restraint.

463
00:48:34,531 --> 00:48:42,657
[SPEAKER_01]: You won't be able to see what the suttas define as grasping the signs and features of sense objects.

464
00:48:42,757 --> 00:48:52,924
[SPEAKER_01]: You won't be able to see that, nor would you be able to see that non-universion that would arise on account of that grasping of signs and features, as the suttas would say.

465
00:48:54,105 --> 00:48:57,087
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, the watchfulness and vigilance

466
00:48:58,099 --> 00:49:05,804
[SPEAKER_01]: Purifying your mind from obstructive states, that would already be fully contingent on seeing the sign of the mind, because otherwise you don't know what obstructive states are.

467
00:49:07,405 --> 00:49:09,526
[SPEAKER_01]: And all the way up to the five hindrances.

468
00:49:09,546 --> 00:49:13,328
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's just refining the same principle of seeing the sign of the mind.

469
00:49:14,169 --> 00:49:20,493
[SPEAKER_01]: And... And it's important to see as well that everyone has that ability to some extent.

470
00:49:20,613 --> 00:49:27,817
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why, for example, in some cases in suttas, these lay people who got enlightened seemingly instantaneously,

471
00:49:29,153 --> 00:49:33,515
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not because of some magical event or luck that they had.

472
00:49:33,555 --> 00:49:58,226
[SPEAKER_01]: It's because, well, for whatever reason, it might be in their past lives or even in their present life, their satiety was not as proliferated, like they were not as constricted in that foreground level of attention, that they were able to see, to some extent, that peripheral domain, the back of your head, as I was saying.

473
00:49:58,895 --> 00:50:05,139
[SPEAKER_01]: And when the Buddha explains these things, they're actually able to see them concretely in their own experience here and now.

474
00:50:05,239 --> 00:50:06,139
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

475
00:50:06,260 --> 00:50:25,431
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if the dhamma doesn't... if hearing about these things, the Four Noble Truths, craving, suffering, cessation of craving, and all these things, and you don't have the right view, that can only be because the sign of the mind, where those recognitions, that would allow those recognitions to land, is not there.

476
00:50:26,211 --> 00:50:27,032
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if you make perfect

477
00:50:33,028 --> 00:50:37,349
[SPEAKER_01]: If you see where that craving is, as I said, always behind you, that's not there.

478
00:50:37,389 --> 00:50:46,012
[SPEAKER_01]: So, even though, yes, you agree with the fact that craving is suffering, because you read about it in the suttas, you won't... it won't lead to the inside of the right view.

479
00:50:46,672 --> 00:50:55,134
[SPEAKER_01]: But, eh... everyone has that recognition at the side of the mind, on a basic level, to some degree, because otherwise you wouldn't be able to even start the practice.

480
00:50:55,754 --> 00:51:00,936
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's just that it not... it's not enough to, eh... to constitute the right view.

481
00:51:01,818 --> 00:51:08,301
[SPEAKER_01]: You could say like, you know, some people can't even catch themselves telling lies.

482
00:51:09,262 --> 00:51:22,988
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, it's just... even if you tell them or whatever, they're just unable to recognize their own actions or not specifically things against the precepts, just anything.

483
00:51:24,115 --> 00:51:35,785
[SPEAKER_01]: They just have... they operate almost like animals, let's say, without recollection and without any simultaneous awareness of the things they're doing while they're doing them.

484
00:51:36,806 --> 00:51:40,209
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that already shows you.

485
00:51:40,249 --> 00:51:52,979
[SPEAKER_01]: And some other people, like, you know, they... very sharp faculties, like even though they haven't heard the dhamma, and they have a natural sense of self-awareness.

486
00:51:55,378 --> 00:52:01,122
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the New Age sense of watching your knees and stuff, because nobody does that unless somebody has told them.

487
00:52:01,222 --> 00:52:08,147
[SPEAKER_01]: Whereas this is something that you would be almost ingrained into, whether you heard dhamma or not, this side of the mind.

488
00:52:08,207 --> 00:52:13,691
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that shows you that it's just a matter of cultivating it further.

489
00:52:13,911 --> 00:52:19,776
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not something that you have to gain for the first time or something like that.

490
00:52:19,976 --> 00:52:21,597
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just furthering

491
00:52:22,624 --> 00:52:23,709
[SPEAKER_01]: Understanding the principle.

492
00:52:23,789 --> 00:52:24,050
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

493
00:52:24,291 --> 00:52:24,934
[SPEAKER_00]: Refining it.

494
00:52:24,974 --> 00:52:25,195
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

495
00:52:25,436 --> 00:52:25,617
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

496
00:52:29,955 --> 00:52:56,358
[SPEAKER_01]: So, a good way to illustrate how seeing the signs of the mind would actually be necessary, even to purify your physical and verbal actions, would be, let's say you have an intention to do something that's externally good, or in a Buddhist sense, good, like visiting a monastery, having dhamma discussions, reading suttas, and then you might assume that that external property of that action is what determines it is wholesome.

497
00:52:57,018 --> 00:53:02,405
[SPEAKER_01]: Failing to see that actually it's the internal motivation behind it that determines it as such.

498
00:53:02,465 --> 00:53:04,948
[SPEAKER_01]: So, and that which can change from day to day.

499
00:53:05,068 --> 00:53:12,397
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if one day you're doing that action because you crave for company, you were bored,

500
00:53:13,155 --> 00:53:14,355
[SPEAKER_01]: You want to have a good time.

501
00:53:14,776 --> 00:53:16,796
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, it's rooted in passion.

502
00:53:17,276 --> 00:53:23,759
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, that same action is actually unwholesome, even though other Buddhists might be praising you in account of it and whatnot.

503
00:53:24,479 --> 00:53:29,080
[SPEAKER_01]: And, it's exactly the same principle on the mental level, which is what the sutta is describing.

504
00:53:29,100 --> 00:53:42,625
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the unskillful bhikkhu assumes that just thinking about the four-sixth pathanas, just contemplating these things and going through the motions, is good, failing to see that if it's driven by passion, aversion, and delusion,

505
00:53:43,354 --> 00:53:48,396
[SPEAKER_01]: If that's the driving force for those contemplations, that whole situation becomes unwholesome.

506
00:53:49,877 --> 00:54:06,964
[SPEAKER_01]: And, eh... you would get to see the signs of your mind in relation to actions on those... on any of those three domains, eh... not through quickness of awareness and developing sharp concentration and being able to catch the sequence of mind moments.

507
00:54:08,279 --> 00:54:11,142
[SPEAKER_01]: But through self-honesty, even on the mental level.

508
00:54:11,522 --> 00:54:20,670
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why if a person doesn't have that basic self-honesty, they can practice the dhamma for decades, they won't get the right view, because they won't be seeing the signs of their mind.

509
00:54:21,391 --> 00:54:25,874
[SPEAKER_01]: And seeing the signs of their mind would result in abandoning the first three fetters,

510
00:54:27,516 --> 00:54:52,653
[SPEAKER_01]: As Anguttara Nikaya 668 says, because, for example, the assumption of virtue and practices, not just rites and rituals, as it's sometimes translated, is precisely rooted in failing to see that even these things that the Buddha factually talked about, the virtue and the beneficial external practices,

511
00:55:03,236 --> 00:55:11,719
[SPEAKER_01]: Obviously, that doesn't mean that one shouldn't be virtuous, because it's more unwholesome not to be virtuous than to be virtuous with a wrong attitude.

512
00:55:12,599 --> 00:55:15,220
[SPEAKER_01]: But it just means that that's not enough in and of itself.

513
00:55:15,340 --> 00:55:22,442
